Squamous Cell Carcinoma (Skin)
Haematology & Oncology
Firm indurated erythematous nodule, Thick adherent keratotic scale, Rapid localized growth, Central ulceration with crust

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-043
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of localized pain and active purulent/bloody discharge from a rapidly growing scalp lesion requiring urgent wound assessment and haemostasis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an elderly patient bleeding continuously from a large, painful, crusted lump on their bald scalp after inadvertently scratching it. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to achieve direct haemostasis, rule out aggressive secondary bacterial infection, and assess the highly suspicious, destructive lesion, preventing significant blood loss and distress overnight.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I scratched this rough scab on my head earlier, and now it is pouring blood and throbbing with a deep, constant ache."
History of Present Illness: The 72-year-old patient reports a 6-week history of a rapid, relentless, and destructive localized growth on their bald scalp, an area of heavy chronic sun exposure. It began as a rough patch and quickly evolved into a firm, highly raised lump with a thick, rough scale. Two hours ago, they inadvertently traumatized the lesion, causing the central crust to dislodge. This resulted in active bleeding and exquisite localized tenderness upon palpation. The patient complains of a new, constant, deep throbbing pain at the site.
Functional Status: Independent; highly anxious and holding a heavily stained towel to their head.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to properly visualize or apply effective pressure to the top of the scalp.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused adult, visibly frustrated and anxious regarding the bleeding scalp wound.
Physical Exam:
Skin: A 2.5 cm firm, deeply indurated, and erythematous nodule is present on the vertex of the bald scalp. The lesion features a thick, adherent, and extremely rough keratotic scale at its periphery. The center is frankly ulcerated with a necrotic, bleeding base. The nodule is exquisitely tender to palpation and feels fixed to the underlying fascia, lacking mobility. Widespread surrounding actinic keratoses are visible.
Lymphatic: Firm, matted, and non-tender regional lymphadenopathy is palpated in the bilateral posterior cervical chains.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm.
Neurological: No focal cranial nerve deficits.
Environmental Observations: Blood-stained towel utilized for pressure.
Medication Reconciliation: No concurrent use of aspirin or oral anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Squamous Cell Carcinoma - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Basal Cell Carcinoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the thick, adherent keratotic scale and rapid, firm indurated growth over weeks, rather than a slow-growing, pearly, translucent papule with prominent arborizing telangiectasias.

Seborrheic Keratosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the exquisite tenderness, deep induration with fixation to underlying tissue, and central necrotic ulceration, which strongly indicate an invasive malignant process rather than a benign, superficial stuck-on plaque.

Infected Epidermoid Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the presence of the thick keratotic scale, the hard indurated borders, and palpable cervical lymphadenopathy indicating a destructive neoplastic growth rather than a simple encapsulated fluid collection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an actively bleeding, deeply indurated, and rapidly expanding scalp nodule featuring a thick keratotic scale and central ulceration. This morphology, combined with the extreme sun exposure history and palpable cervical lymphadenopathy, is highly characteristic of an advanced, invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma. The friable, necrotic base of the tumour prevents normal physiological haemostasis. Delaying assessment until daytime hours would expose the patient to continuous, unmanageable haemorrhage, severe localized pain, and extreme psychological distress. The urgent after-hours visit was essential to physically achieve mechanical haemostasis, apply specialized dressings, and initiate an immediate rapid-access surgical referral pathway.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of an alginate haemostatic dressing and a firm, compressive tubular bandage to the scalp achieved complete haemostasis. Generated an immediate, urgent referral to the rapid-access plastic surgery/dermatology clinic for an urgent full-thickness skin biopsy anticipating pleomorphic squamous cells invading the dermis, and wide local excision.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Leave the firm tubular bandage completely untouched for the next 48 hours. By preventing any mechanical friction or disruption of the new clot, you eliminate the trigger for recurrent, heavy bleeding from the fragile tumour bed.

Sleep strictly propped up on two pillows tonight. By keeping your head elevated above your heart, you lower the local venous blood pressure in your scalp, directly reducing the painful throbbing sensation and the risk of bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 48 hours to inspect the dressing and ensure the specialist surgical appointment is confirmed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the bleeding restarts and heavily soaks through the entire thickness of the tubular bandage, forming active drips that cannot be stopped by pressing firmly on the outside of the dressing for 15 minutes.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical appearance of this rapidly growing, hard lump strongly suggests a serious skin cancer; a surgical biopsy is absolutely required to confirm this under a microscope.
Clinical Course: The bleeding has been stopped with a specialized clotting dressing, but the underlying tissue is destructive and fragile. Prompt surgical removal is required to cure the lesion and stop it from spreading to the lymph nodes.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not remove the head dressing tonight. If heavy bleeding resumes, apply firm pressure directly over the top of the bandage and proceed to the ED.

